**JavaScript – Set → unique → sorted**

```js
// Input array (you can replace this with your own data)
const nums = [3, 1, 2, 1, 4, 3];

// Step‑by‑step:
//   1️⃣ Convert to a `Set` – automatically removes duplicates.
//   2️⃣ Turn the `Set` back into an ordinary array (`Array.from` or `[...set]`).  
//   3️⃣ Sort numerically (`Array.prototype.sort()` needs a compare fn).
//
const uniqueAndSorted = [...new Set(nums)].sort((a, b) => a - b);

console.log(uniqueAndSorted);
// → [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

### Why this works

| Operation | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| **`new Set(array)`** | Keeps only one copy of each value because sets cannot contain duplicate keys. |
| **Spread / `Array.from(set)`** | Turns the set back into a plain array so you can call `.sort()`. |
| **`.sort((a, b) => a - b)`** | Default `Array.prototype.sort()` sorts strings lexicographically. Passing `(a, b) => a - b` forces numerical comparison, giving true ascending order. |

### Complexity

* Building the set: O(*n*) time, O(*k*) space (*k* = number of distinct values).  
* Sorting: O(*k* log *k*) time, O(*k*) extra space (in-place sort on the resulting array).

Overall: **O(*n* + *k* log *k*)** time, **O(*k*)** auxiliary memory.

---

#### Alternative (if you prefer functional style)

```js
const uniqueAndSorted =
    Array.from(
        new Map(nums.map(v => [v, null]))      // map keeps first occurrence
    ).map(([value]) => Number(value))
     .sort((a, b) => a - b);
```

But the simple `Set` approach above is usually clearer and just as efficient for most cases.
